<p>This chapter is short on purpose. The language design for concurrency is written, but the compiler does not run it yet. Every program you have seen in this book runs one step at a time. This page says what is planned, shows what the compiler says today if you try, and gives you the habits that will carry over.</p>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. Everything on this page about scopes, spawned tasks, joining, mutexes and channels.</note>

<h2 id="today">What happens if you try</h2>
<p>Earlier drafts used <code>async</code> functions and <code>await</code> to wait for them. Revision 0019 replaced that design with structured concurrency: <code>scope</code> blocks and <code>spawn</code>. Neither the old keywords nor the new design run in the compiler today. If you try the keywords from earlier drafts, both are rejected while parsing:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">async i64 fetch(i64 n) {
    return n + 1
}

void main() uses console {
    print($"{await fetch(1)}")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1002:</span> expected an expression, found keyword `async`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> async-try.byn:1:1

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> <span class="hl">async</span> i64 fetch(i64 n) {
<span class="gut">    │</span> <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>

<span class="err">error BYN1002:</span> expected an expression, found keyword `await`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> async-try.byn:6:14

<span class="gut">  6 │</span>     print($"{<span class="hl">await</span> fetch(1)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>              <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 2 errors, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN1002</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened to <code>async-try.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The error tells you the parser rejects the keywords from earlier drafts. The new design is not implemented yet: <code>scope</code> and <code>spawn</code> are not recognized, and types like <code>Mutex</code> and <code>Channel</code> are unknown types (BYN2002).</p>
<p>Two parts are accepted but do nothing yet. A function may say <code>uses suspend</code> in its effect list (see <a href="book-14-effects.html">chapter 14</a>), and a file may start with <code>#:io evented</code>. Nothing can suspend, so neither changes how your program runs.</p>

<h2 id="design">The planned shape</h2>
<p>The design has three ideas.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Waiting is an effect.</strong> A function that can suspend says <code>uses suspend</code>. Because it is in the signature, you can see from a function's header whether it can wait.</li>
<li><strong>Tasks live in a scope.</strong> You spawn tasks inside a <code>scope</code> block with <code>spawn</code>. The scope waits for every child before it exits, so no task outlives its caller. You wait for a task with <code>.join()</code>. Leaving the scope cancels child tasks that are still running, and children may borrow from the caller's stack.</li>
<li><strong>Shared data has rules.</strong> Shared mutation goes through <code>Mutex&lt;T&gt;</code>, atomics, or channels. Holding a mutex guard across a suspend point is a compile error, and passing <code>ref x</code> to a spawned task locks <code>x</code> in the parent until the scope ends.</li>
</ol>
<pre><code class="byn">// planned
i64 prepareIftar(String host) uses suspend fails NetError {
    return 10
}

i64 totalMeals() uses suspend fails NetError {
    scope(timeout: 5s) {
        let aisha = spawn prepareIftar("Aisha")
        let bilal = spawn prepareIftar("Bilal")
        return try aisha.join() + try bilal.join()
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>Treat that fragment as a sketch of the design, not as something to copy. The <a href="cookbook-concurrency.html">concurrency cookbook</a> lists each planned form with its place in the specification.</p>

<h2 id="habits">Habits that already carry over</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep data owned by one place and pass it by <code>move</code> or <code>ref</code> (<a href="book-13-memory.html">chapter 13</a>). The rules for tasks build on the same rules.</li>
<li>Declare effects in <code>uses</code> clauses (<a href="book-14-effects.html">chapter 14</a>). Waiting will be one more effect in the same list.</li>
<li>For parallel work today, run several processes. The <a href="cookbook-processes.html">processes cookbook</a> shows how.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>1. You write <code>async i64 fetch(i64 n)</code> from an earlier draft. What does the compiler report today?
<ol type="a"><li>A run-time panic.</li><li>BYN1002, expected an expression, found keyword <code>async</code>.</li><li>Nothing, the function runs as ordinary code.</li></ol></summary>
<p>b. The keyword was part of an earlier draft and is rejected by the parser today. The design replaced <code>async</code> and <code>await</code> with <code>scope</code> and <code>spawn</code>.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>2. You add <code>uses suspend</code> to a function. Does it now run concurrently?</summary>
<p>No. The effect is accepted in a declaration, but nothing can suspend yet. It only records in the signature that the function may wait.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>3. Why does the design say a task may borrow from its caller's stack?</summary>
<p>Because the scope waits for every child before it exits, no child task can outlive the borrow. This matches the rule from chapter 13: a borrow must not outlive what it points into.</p></details>

